<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Direct Variation to Solve Application Problems, Part 2</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00336</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Using Direct Variation to Solve Application Problems, Part 2</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b816cdff-5464-495b-ad18-3650a37e0185</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Jesse’s car gets 30 miles per gallon of gas.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Write an equation using direct variation for distance, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and number of gallons of gas, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> for this problem.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00004">Use the equation for direct variation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">If Jesse travels 150 miles he uses 5 gallons of gas. Find the constant of variation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00007">Use the equation for direct variation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The constant of variation is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">Now, write the equation if Jesse travels <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> miles with a car that uses 30 miles per gallon of gas.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00010"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">If Las Vegas is 285 miles away, how many gallons of gas are needed to get there?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00013">Substitute 285 miles in for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> in the equation. So, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>285</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
        
          <media alt="A woman and a young girl smile while holding a piggy bank together, as seen on a tablet screen held by a hand. The background includes a kitchen with warm lighting and a brick wall.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_17_09_01.png" width="300"/>
          </media>
        
        <para id="para-00014">Alex's mother can use algebra to budget her paycheck from her job as an accountant. She uses some money for food, rent, medicine, and things like water and electricity.</para>
        <para id="para-00015">If she uses variables to represent the different ways that she uses her money, she can put those variables into an equation to make sure she has enough money to pay for all those things.</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>